        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Ranked Them</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills. The ranking is built from Horizon's published
            specifications and warranty terms, a listing from Horizon's parent company's own retail
            chain, and independent review coverage, all checked on 13 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started by confirming what Horizon actually sells. Its US treadmill page listed five
            models, and each product page carried a full specification table we could read
            directly: deck, belt thickness, speed, incline, step-on height, weight, console,
            connectivity and warranty. We then compared those figures with independent coverage of
            the range and, for the 7.4 AT, with a Johnson Fitness &amp; Wellness store listing.
            Where everything agreed, the figure went into the spec line.
          </p>
          <p>
            One figure never made it, on any model: motor horsepower. Horizon describes its drive
            as a RapidSync motor on the Studio Series and a Johnson Drive System on the Go Series,
            and lists the thrust of the incline motor, but it does not publish a horsepower or
            continuous-duty rating for any current treadmill. Reviewers quote figures from 2.5 to
            4.0 horsepower across the range, and some of them say openly that the number comes
            from their own research rather than from Horizon. A number nobody can trace back to
            the manufacturer is not one we print as fact.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also checked the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Horizon
            treadmills. It lists a 2022 recall of T101-05 treadmills and a 2002 recall of much older
            models; we found nothing for the 7.0 AT, 7.4 AT, 7.8 AT or T202.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ranking weighs four things in order: the deck and weight rating, because they decide
            who the machine suits; the parts and labour warranty, because the lifetime frame and
            motor cover is the same everywhere and the shorter terms are where models differ; the
            price at which each is actually sold, which on Horizon's store is well below the
            reference price; and stock, last, because it changes week to week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="no-subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The No-Subscription Case, and Its Limits</h2>
<p>
            Horizon's whole proposition is what it leaves out. NordicTrack and ProForm build their
            machines around iFIT, Peloton around its own membership, Echelon around its app. Each
            of those platforms is a recurring fee, and each machine is designed so that the screen
            and the automatic speed and incline control are worth much less once you stop paying.
            Horizon does not have a platform. Its consoles are data displays with built-in
            programs, and the connected part of the machine is a Bluetooth radio that sends your
            speed, incline and distance to whichever app you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            That radio uses Bluetooth FTMS, the open fitness machine standard, and every current
            Horizon lists it. In practice that means the machine works with Zwift, the Peloton
            app, Kinomap and similar apps on your own phone, tablet or laptop, and you can change
            apps without changing treadmills. Horizon also lists Strava and other tracking apps.
            Only the T101 Connect lists JRNY, the adaptive-workout service that Horizon's parent
            company, Johnson Health Tech, acquired with Bowflex in 2024 — and it is optional, like
            everything else here.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a trade-off, and it is worth being clear about. Because the apps receive data
            from a Horizon rather than control it, a class or a Zwift route cannot raise and lower
            the incline for you. When the road climbs, you turn the QuickDial or EasyDial yourself.
            On a machine designed around dials rather than repeated button presses, that is quick,
            but it is not the same as an iFIT machine that follows the trainer automatically. If
            automatic control is what keeps you training, a Horizon is the wrong choice, and our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart treadmill roundup</a>
            covers the machines built around each platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            For everyone else, the arithmetic is simple. A subscription that costs a few hundred
            dollars a year adds up to more than the difference between a T202 and a 7.8 AT over the
            life of the machine. Horizon puts that money into the frame and the warranty instead,
            and a Horizon with no app at all is still a complete treadmill: the programs, the dials
            and the heart rate receiver all work out of the box. Our roundup of
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            compares Horizon with the other brands that take the same approach, and if Zwift is the
            app you have in mind, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for Zwift</a>
            guide ranks the 7.0 AT and 7.8 AT against the alternatives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="series-compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Go Series vs Studio Series</h2>
<p>
            Horizon's two series are aimed at different users, and choosing the series first
            narrows the decision to one or two machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Go Series: the walking tier.</strong> The T101 Connect and T202 are lighter,
            lower and simpler. They weigh 180 and 187 lb, have the lowest step-on heights in the
            range at about seven inches, and use a thinner 1.4 mm belt. The T101 Connect has a
            55-inch deck, 10 mph and 10% incline; the T202 a 60-inch deck, 12 mph and 12%. Consoles
            are LED windows on the T101 and a 5-inch LCD on the T202, with EasyDial controls on the
            handgrips. Both carry one year on parts and labour. They suit walking, incline walking
            and some jogging, and they fold more easily than the Studio models simply because there
            is less of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Studio Series: the running tier.</strong> The 7.0 AT, 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT all run
            to 12 mph and 15% incline on a 60-inch deck, with thicker rollers, QuickDial controls,
            custom interval keys and a fan. They step up in width, capacity and warranty: 20-inch
            belt and 325 lb on the 7.0 AT, 22-inch belt and 350 lb on the 7.4 AT, 22-inch belt,
            thicker 2.2 mm belt and 375 lb on the 7.8 AT. Parts cover is three years on the 7.0 AT
            and five on the other two, and labour one and two years respectively. They weigh 277 to
            330 lb, so although they fold, they are machines that stay in one room.
          </p>
          <p>
            A rough rule: walk mostly, choose the Go Series; run at all regularly, choose the
            Studio Series. Within the Studio Series, the 7.0 AT is enough for one runner of average
            build, the 7.4 AT is the right step for a broader runner or a household that shares the
            machine, and the 7.8 AT is for the heaviest users and the heaviest use. If you are
            between sizes, our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains when two extra inches of width are worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Reading Horizon’s Warranty</h2>
<p>
            Every current Horizon treadmill carries a lifetime warranty on the frame and the motor
            for home use. That is generous at these prices — many brands selling machines under
            $1,000 cover the motor for a few years — and it is part of the reason Horizon is so
            often recommended to people who want to buy once. But because it is the same on every
            model, it does not help you choose between them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts and labour are where the range divides.</strong> The T101 Connect and T202
            have one year on parts and one year on labour. The 7.0 AT has three years on parts and
            one on labour. The 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT have five years on parts and two on labour. Parts
            cover is the term most likely to matter in practice, because the components that wear
            or fail on a treadmill in its first few years — the console electronics, the incline
            motor, the belt and deck, the speed sensor — are parts, not frame or motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Labour is short everywhere.</strong> One or two years of labour means that once
            it runs out, a technician's visit is at your expense even when the part is free. That is
            normal in this market, but it is worth knowing before you assume a lifetime warranty
            means a lifetime of free repairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 30-day guarantee.</strong> Horizon's product pages also describe a 30-day
            return for a full refund. Use it properly: run the machine at the speeds and inclines
            you actually plan to use in the first fortnight, listen for noise from the rollers,
            and check that the belt tracks straight. Confirm the current return terms, including
            who pays for collection, before you order. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read component terms like these across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-details" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Stock, Pricing and Setup</h2>
<p>
            A few practical points are worth knowing before a Horizon arrives, and one of them is
            whether it will arrive this month at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stock moves.</strong> On 13 October 2026 Horizon's store showed the 7.0 AT ready
            to ship, the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT shipping the week of 26 October, the T202 the week of
            16 November, and the T101 Connect sold out. Ship dates of a few weeks are common in
            autumn, so a later date is not a reason to switch to a model that suits you less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never pay the reference price.</strong> Horizon shows a regular price and a
            sale price on every model, and the sale price is the one it sells at. On the day we
            checked, promotional codes took a further $100 to $300 off the Studio Series and the
            T202. Prices on Amazon and at other retailers can differ from Horizon's own store, so
            compare before ordering.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the circuit.</strong> Horizon's specification tables say its treadmills
            need a 120V outlet and are not compatible with GFCI outlets, which are common in
            garages, basements and bathrooms. A GFCI outlet can trip when the motor starts. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cord and circuit guide</a>
            explains why a treadmill wants its own grounded outlet and not a power strip.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Measure for the fold and the step.</strong> Studio Series machines measure about
            76 inches long assembled and 42 to 44 inches folded, and stand up to 71 inches tall
            when folded. Step-on heights run from about seven inches on the Go Series to 8.75 inches
            on the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT. Add your height and a margin for the bounce of running, and
            check the result against the ceiling, especially in a basement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Which Horizon Treadmills to Skip</h2>
<p>
            Several Horizon names still turn up in searches, on marketplace listings and in older
            reviews, and not all of them are worth buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Models not on Horizon's US site.</strong> The T303, the Elite and Evolve models
            and older Paragon versions were not listed on horizonfitness.com on 13 October 2026.
            The 5.0 AT and the Paragon X are still sold by retailers in Europe and Australia, but
            not through Horizon's US store, and their specifications and warranties differ by
            region. A US warranty claim on a machine Horizon does not sell here is a conversation
            to have before buying, not after.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Used or older T101s without a serial check.</strong> The 2022 recall covered
            about 192,000 T101-05 treadmills sold in the US with serial numbers beginning TM734 or
            TM486. The fix is a free software update from Horizon, but a second-hand seller may
            never have installed it. Check the serial number and ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The 7.8 AT, for most people.</strong> It shares its speed, incline, deck size
            and warranty with the 7.4 AT. Unless you need the extra 25 lb of capacity or will run
            on it every day, the 7.4 AT is the same machine for less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The T202, if you run often.</strong> Its one-year parts cover and thinner belt
            are the price of its low cost. If you run several times a week, the 7.0 AT is usually
            only a couple of hundred dollars more on sale, and its three years of parts cover alone
            justify the difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Any of them, if you want classes to drive the machine.</strong> Horizon is the
            right brand only if you are content to set the speed and incline yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy a Different Brand</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want automatic incline from classes.</strong> Horizon machines report to
            apps but are not controlled by them. NordicTrack's iFIT machines are; our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack roundup</a>
            ranks the current range, with the subscription as an ongoing cost.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want decline or steep incline.</strong> No current Horizon declines, and
            all of them top out at 15% or less. If downhill training or steep hiking practice is
            the point, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with decline</a>
            guide covers the machines that go below zero.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 350 lb.</strong> The 7.8 AT's 375 lb rating is the
            highest Horizon offers, and a rating is a ceiling rather than a comfortable operating
            point. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity treadmill guide</a>
            covers machines rated higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want the closest rival.</strong> The Bowflex T6, now made by the same
            parent company, lines up almost exactly with the 7.0 AT on deck, speed, incline and
            capacity, and is often similarly priced; our
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6 review</a>
            sets out its confirmed figures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Horizon treadmill is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the 7.0 AT. It has a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, a 325 lb rating, Bluetooth FTMS for apps, and three years of parts cover on top of a lifetime frame and motor warranty. Choose the 7.4 AT for a wider deck and longer cover, the 7.8 AT for the highest weight rating, and the T202 if you mainly walk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Horizon treadmills need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Horizon has no content platform of its own. Every current model works fully with its built-in programs and dials, and sends speed and incline over Bluetooth to apps such as Zwift and the Peloton app. Those apps may charge their own fees, and the T101 Connect also supports the optional paid JRNY service, but the treadmill needs none of them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Zwift or Peloton control a Horizon treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Horizon treadmills send speed, incline and distance to apps over Bluetooth FTMS, but the apps cannot change the speed or incline. When a route climbs or a class instructor calls for a change, you turn the dial yourself. If automatic control matters to you, an iFIT machine from NordicTrack or ProForm is designed for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon T101 still available?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 13 October 2026 Horizon listed the T101 Connect on its US store but showed it as sold out at $699, with no restock date. Older T101-05 units with serial numbers beginning TM734 or TM486 were recalled in 2022 for a software fix, so check the serial number on any older or second-hand unit before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Horizon 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly build and capacity. Both have a 22 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, 15% incline, five years on parts and two of labour. The 7.8 AT adds a 375 lb rating against 350 lb, a thicker 2.2 mm belt, a 9.3-inch colour display and aluminium side rails. For most runners the 7.4 AT is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does a Horizon treadmill have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon describes a RapidSync drive on its Studio Series and a Johnson Drive System on its Go Series, but it does not publish a horsepower rating for any current treadmill. Reviewers quote figures from 2.5 to 4.0 horsepower, which we could not confirm with Horizon. Judge the machines on deck, capacity and warranty instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Horizon</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from treating every Horizon as the same machine because they share a warranty headline and a software approach.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the lifetime warranty as complete cover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Lifetime cover applies to the frame and motor only. Parts run from one year on the Go Series to five on the 7.4 AT and 7.8 AT, and labour from one to two years. Compare those terms, because they are where the models genuinely differ.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting apps to drive the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon treadmills report to Zwift, Peloton and similar apps but are not controlled by them. If you want a class to change your incline for you, buy a machine designed for that and budget for its subscription.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Go Series machine to run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T101 Connect and T202 are built for walking and jogging, with thinner belts and one-year parts cover. Regular runners are better served by the 7.0 AT, which is often only a couple of hundred dollars more on sale.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into a GFCI outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon says its treadmills are not compatible with GFCI outlets, which are common in garages and basements. Check the outlet before delivery day, and give the treadmill its own grounded circuit rather than a power strip.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

